\section{Standard errors}{

Every fitted object carries `se_type`, which states where its standard errors
come from.  The competing estimators report genuine sandwich standard errors;
the proposed estimator reports an influence-function standard error.  The two
are never conflated.
}

\section{Nuisance estimation}{

\NTC{For a continuous outcome, \code{SIV()} estimates the baseline density
\eqn{f(Y \mid A=0,Z=0,X)} with the default \code{np_gss_cv} ensemble.
Likelihood-CV \code{np} and \code{gss} density candidates are combined by
minimizing inner-validation MSPE over nonnegative weights summing to one.
MSPE denotes mean
squared prediction error, also known as integrated squared error (ISE).
The \code{np_normal} and \code{np_cv} alternatives use only \code{np}.}
Its remaining regression nuisances use the Super Learner ensemble of
[MySL()]. [SIV_LATE()] retains its own regression
learner interface but does not require a conditional-density nuisance.
}

\section{Density-ratio estimation}{

\NTC{For a continuous outcome the fixed point needs the density ratio
\eqn{R_Y}. The default KLIEP/uLSIF ensemble combines reconstructed
conditional densities using MSPE alone. Its loss criterion is fixed.
\code{ratio_bandwidth = "cv_separate"} selects separate outcome and
standardized-covariate bandwidths by cross-validation. Standalone KLIEP
and uLSIF fits remain available through \code{ratio_method}.}
}

